import sys
from pathlib import Path
TD=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;sys.path.insert(0,str(TD.parent))
from manuscript_support import build
E=[
('01-00','Two classifications','Traditional and modern describe broad educational knowledge and institutions, whereas formal, informal and non-formal describe how learning is organised.','They are different axes. Traditional knowledge can be taught in a formal course, and contemporary digital skills can be learned informally.'),
('01-01','Traditional education','Traditional education is learning rooted in a community’s inherited knowledge, practices and values; this classification differs from formal versus non-formal provision.','Learning through demonstration and participation can transmit relevant knowledge. Practices vary and adapt; traditional does not mean unchanged or without reasoning.'),
('01-02','Modern education','Modern education is education shaped by contemporary institutions and knowledge, including formal systems; distinguish this broad contrast with traditional education from the separate formal/informal/non-formal classification.','It can extend access to scientific and technical knowledge but needs locally usable resources and inclusive provision.'),
('02-00','Compare strengths and limits','Community-rooted learning can offer relevance and participation, while institutional education can offer broader curriculum and recognised qualifications.','Either may exclude people or miss useful knowledge. Compare suitability for the learner’s goal, access and quality rather than assuming one wholly replaces the other.'),
('03-00','Organisation of learning','Formal education operates within the recognised structured education system; non-formal education is organised outside its main system, while informal learning occurs through everyday experience.','The distinction concerns organisation, not quality or whether teaching uses old or new knowledge. An adult course may be non-formal but carefully planned.'),
('03-01','Formal education','Formal education is education organised within the recognised, structured education system, normally with planned stages and qualifications.','A school course illustrates it. Whether its teaching is effective requires separate evidence about access, learning and support.'),
('03-02','Informal education','Informal education is learning through everyday experience and interaction outside a planned education programme.','Learning a household repair through daily interaction may illustrate it. Informal does not mean worthless or necessarily inaccurate.'),
('03-03','Non-formal education','Non-formal education is organised learning outside the main formal education system, such as a planned adult skills programme.','It can fit adult work schedules, but needs suitable teaching and resources. It differs from unplanned everyday learning.'),
('04-00','Measure provision','Literacy, school enrolment and tertiary participation describe different aspects of educational provision.','Use age group, denominator and period consistently. Enrolment is not attendance, completion or learning quality, and a larger count can reflect a larger eligible population.'),
('04-01','Literacy','A literacy measure identifies reading and writing ability under the source’s stated definition and population.','Compare the same age and measurement rules. A national average can hide group or regional gaps and does not measure every skill.'),
('04-02','School enrolment','Primary and secondary enrolment counts show participation registered at those levels.','Compare counts with eligible populations or capacity and keep levels separate. More registered learners do not establish regular attendance or adequate teaching.'),
('04-03','Tertiary enrolment','Tertiary enrolment measures participation in post-secondary institutions under the source’s definition.','A rising total can indicate expansion while its rate remains unchanged if the eligible population grows similarly. Access, course relevance and completion need separate evidence.'),
('05-00','ICT and access','ICT can support learning materials, communication and flexible access, but requires usable devices, power, connectivity, skills and suitable content.','It may widen opportunities or deepen a digital gap. Learning gains need evidence; distributing devices alone does not establish teaching or access.'),
('06-00','Alternative routes','Non-formal courses, vocational education and lifelong learning can provide different routes to skills and continued participation.','Their development contribution depends on relevance, access and opportunities to use learning. A certificate alone cannot create demand for a skill.'),
('06-02','Vocational education','Vocational education develops practical knowledge and skills relevant to particular occupations or work activities.','It can fit a documented skill gap when tools, training quality and work opportunities align; a narrow course with no usable equipment or demand offers weaker evidence of benefit.'),
('06-03','Lifelong learning','Lifelong learning continues across life through different formal, non-formal and informal opportunities.','It can help people adapt to change but needs time, access and support. Adult responsibilities should inform the arrangement rather than be treated as lack of motivation.'),
('07-00','Education and HIV','Accurate education can counter HIV misinformation, reduce stigma and help people understand prevention and qualified support routes.','It should protect privacy and continued participation. Sharing classrooms, food or ordinary contact does not transmit HIV; no learner should be asked to disclose status in a class exercise.')]
L={s:(m,e) for s,h,m,e in E};Q={}
def q(n,p,a,g):Q[n]=(p,a,g)
q(1,'Differentiate traditional/modern education from formal/non-formal classification.','The first concerns broad knowledge and institutions; the second concerns organisation within or outside the recognised system. Traditional knowledge may be taught formally.',['Comparison: separate the two axes.'])
q(2,'Suggest a learning arrangement for an invented class needing both local environmental knowledge and methods for testing water observations.','Combine consent-based local demonstrations with a structured investigation and recording method. This uses relevant knowledge while developing an explicit evidence-testing procedure.',['Context: two stated learning needs.','Reason: complementary learning rather than a false either/or.'])
q(25,'Explain the error in saying a structured school lesson about inherited farming knowledge must be non-formal education.','The content is traditional knowledge, but its organisation can be formal. Test the knowledge/system contrast separately from organisation within or outside formal schooling.',['Error: content tradition substitutes for organisational category.','Distinction: two independent classifications.'])
for n,s in [(3,'01-01'),(4,'01-02'),(9,'03-01'),(10,'03-02'),(11,'03-03')]:q(n,'Define '+dict((x[0],x[1]) for x in E)[s].lower()+'.',L[s][0],['Meaning: '+L[s][0]])
q(5,'Compare one access feature of community demonstration and a distant institution requiring daily travel.','A local demonstration may be easier to reach, while the distant institution imposes travel needs; actual quality and goals remain separate questions.',['Comparison: pair the same access dimension.'])
q(6,'Describe the fictional alternatives and give a reasoned judgement for a learner needing a recognised technical qualification: local demonstrations offer relevant experience but no qualification; a reachable funded course offers practice and the required qualification.','The course better matches the stated goal because access and practical provision accompany recognition. Demonstrations can add relevant experience but do not supply the required qualification; they may complement the course.',['Judgement: assess against the explicit qualification goal.','Reason: weigh relevance, access and recognition from both alternatives.'])
q(7,'Differentiate non-formal and informal education.','Non-formal learning is organised outside the main formal system; informal learning occurs through everyday experience outside a planned programme.',['Comparison: planned programme versus everyday learning.'])
q(8,'Suggest a learning route where invented adult workers need planned tool training outside school and can attend only evenings.','Provide a suitable non-formal evening programme with safe practical instruction and resources. It matches the time constraint and need for organised training.',['Context: adult timetable and practical need.','Reason: fit the route to access and instruction.'])
q(12,'Calculate literacy percentage if 420 of 600 people in a fictional defined age group meet the stated test.','420 ÷ 600 = 0.7; multiplying by 100 gives 70%.',['Working: meeting definition divided by group tested.','Unit: percent of the stated age group.'])
data={13:('A registers 300 of 500 eligible school children; B registers 240 of 300','A has more enrolled children but B the higher rate, 80% versus 60%. Counts and coverage give different rankings.'),14:('literacy is 80% in both areas, but A’s rural group is 50% while B’s is 75%','Equal overall averages conceal a lower rural literacy figure in A, 25 percentage points below B; inspect group composition rather than infer equal access.'),15:('primary enrolment rises from 400 of 500 eligible to 540 of 600','Count and rate both rise: 140 more learners and coverage from 80% to 90%. This supports wider registration, not proof of completion or learning.'),16:('tertiary enrolment rises from 100 of 1,000 eligible to 150 of 1,500','The count rises by 50 but the rate remains 10%. Expansion in numbers has kept pace with the eligible group rather than increased its participation share.')}
for n,(case,a) in data.items():q(n,'Describe the provision relationship in this original fictional comparison: '+case+'.',a,['Evidence: accurate use of counts and denominators or subgroup figures.','Relationship: '+a])
q(17,'Explain the learning-access mechanism where an invented remote school receives devices but has no reliable power or teacher preparation for the material.','Devices alone are not usable provision: power interrupts access and teachers lack the support to integrate the resources. Capacity to teach and use the material is needed before learning gains are established.',['Mechanism: use both support gaps.','Consequence: equipment does not establish accessible learning.'])
q(18,'Explain which fictional ICT option fits accessible learning: connected devices with teacher training and accessible materials, or more devices without power or content support.','The supported option better connects equipment to usable learning. More unsupported devices increase stock but leave essential access and teaching conditions absent; compare actual reach and quality after implementation.',['Judgement: choose against usable learning.','Reason: weigh quantity with working support.'])
q(19,'Explain how an alternative education route can support sustainable development.','Relevant accessible learning can build skills for productive work and resource decisions. People need opportunities and means to apply those skills; attendance or a certificate alone does not establish the benefit.',['Reason: learning through usable capability to a development outcome.'])
q(20,'Explain which fictional adult programme fits a skill-and-work aim: A offers a fashionable certificate without practice or local demand; B offers relevant practical training with equipment and identified opportunities.','B better matches usable capability and work. A’s certificate may offer recognition, but lacks evidence of skill practice and demand; the comparison should track application and outcomes.',['Judgement: select against relevant skill use.','Reason: weigh recognition with actual practice and opportunity.'])
q(21,'Explain whether a vocational repair course is suitable when fictional local workshops report vacancies but the course has no tools. A funded equipment partnership is feasible.','The skill aim matches demand, but tool access is needed for practical competence. The partnership can make the course viable if training and maintenance are provided; vacancies alone cannot replace practice.',['Judgement: conditional suitability.','Reason: weigh demand with the missing practical input and feasible remedy.'])
q(22,'Explain which lifelong-learning arrangement fits fictional shift workers: daytime-only classes or supported sessions at workable times with the same learning standard.','Workable supported sessions better fit access without lowering the standard. Daytime-only provision may be simpler to organise but excludes learners whose shifts conflict; monitor actual participation and learning.',['Judgement: compare access and quality.','Reason: use the stated time constraint.'])
q(23,'Explain how accurate HIV education can reduce exclusion from learning.','Correcting misinformation about ordinary social contact can remove a false basis for excluding classmates. Privacy and safe qualified support routes support participation without asking learners to disclose status.',['Reason: connect accurate information to reduced stigma and access.'])
q(24,'Explain the access mechanism in a fictional school where learners believe sharing desks transmits HIV and affected classmates are being avoided.','The belief is incorrect, and avoidance adds an unnecessary participation barrier. Accurate information, respectful behaviour and private qualified support can address misinformation without exposing anyone’s status.',['Mechanism: misinformation linked to exclusion.','Consequence: correct the false basis while protecting participation and privacy.'])
P={'P01':(['03-00','06-00'],'State (a) the distinction between formal and non-formal education [2]. Explain (b) why a fictional evening skills programme with tools can be more usable to working adults than daytime-only provision, even if both have equal admission rules [4].','(a) Within the recognised structured system versus organised learning outside its main system. (b) Evening timing removes the work conflict and tools allow practice; written admission alone supplies neither usable time nor practical capacity.',['Point: organisational distinction [2].','Development: use both timing and tools to explain access and skill development [4].']),
'P02':(['04-00','04-02'],'Calculate (a) enrolment coverage for fictional A with 360 enrolled among 600 eligible [2]. Explain (b) whether A offers broader access than B with 300 among 400, and whether rates alone prove teaching quality [4].','(a) 360 ÷ 600 = 0.6; multiplying by 100 gives 60%. (b) B has 75% coverage despite fewer learners, so A’s larger count does not show broader registration. Neither rate establishes attendance, learning or teaching quality, which need separate evidence.',['Evidence: correct denominator and 60% [2].','Meaning: compare B’s 75% and bound the quality inference [4].'])}
examples=[(n,'Apply the distinction',Q[k][0]+' '+Q[k][1],[s for s in L if s.startswith(n+'-')]) for n,k in [('01',25),('02',6),('03',8),('04',13),('05',17),('06',21),('07',24)]]
examples.append(('07','Health information source','The [WHO HIV fact sheet](https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/hiv-aids), checked 20 October 2026, supports the ordinary-contact distinction used here. Educational inclusion is not an individual clinical assessment, and classmates should not be asked to investigate or disclose health status.',['07-00']))
links={n:'Connect to Health (7.2), modern roles (6.2) and Development (1.1). Separate access, participation and learning outcomes; do not assume a system label or device purchase establishes quality.' for n in ['01','02','03','04','05','06','07']}
if __name__=='__main__':build(TD,E,Q,P,links,examples)
